<main class="wrap">

  <p class="kicker">Part V · Will it last? · Chapter 28</p>
  <h1>Knowing When to Stop</h1>
  <p class="lede">The last decision is the one the book began with. <a href="04-deciding-not-to.html">Chapter
  four</a> asked you to write a stopping rule before you had anything to lose; this chapter is
  about the version of that rule nobody writes — the one for a business that has run for years,
  employed people, taken money from a bank or a family or investors, and has quietly run out of
  moves. Stopping it is a decision with a date and a procedure. Almost nobody is taught either.</p>

  <div class="decision">
    <span class="tag">The decision</span>
    Whether the business has exhausted its options or only your ideas for the month; when to have
    the conversation that turns <em>what more can we do?</em> into <em>it is time</em>; and how to
    stop in the order that leaves the least behind — what can be sold, what is owed, what is
    filed, and to whom you account — rather than drifting until the stop is made for you.
  </div>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 1</span> The situation</h2>

    <p>It has not failed. That is what makes it hard. Customers still pay, the people still come
    in, the bank still clears the wages most months. What has happened is quieter: the last big
    move did not move anything, the one before it did not either, and the weekly conversation
    with yourself or your partner has had the same single item on it for longer than you would
    admit — <em>what more can we do?</em> Every answer costs another quarter, and every quarter
    spends something you will want later: the reserve from <a href="23-slack.html">chapter
    twenty-three</a>, the goodwill of the people you would have to let go, the price the assets
    would fetch while they are still assets.</p>

    <p>The stopping rule chapter four gave you was for the start, when the number was small and every consequence on its menu was cheap — a salary to go back
    to, a domain to keep. This is the rule for the end, and it is harder for
    the same reason it is more important: there is more to lose by stopping, and more to lose by
    not. <strong>The question is not whether the business can go on. It usually can, for a while.
    It is whether going on is a decision or the absence of one</strong> — and if you cannot say
    what would have to happen, by when, for the answer to be <em>stop</em>, it is the absence.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 2</span> What actually happened</h2>

    <p>Before Gymdesk, Eran Galperin had another company, and this is its ending. Binpress, a marketplace for developers' code libraries, was
    conceived in late 2010 by Galperin and his business partner, Adam, out of a web-development
    shop they had run for four years; it soft-launched at the end of that year, and <em>"Over the
    course of 2 months, we had exactly 10 new users."</em> A contest with $40,000 in sponsored
    prizes seeded the marketplace with around two hundred products; sales then ran at <em>"around $500 in sales"</em> a month for <em>"the first several
    months"</em>. Mobile components changed that —
    <em>"Our revenue stabilized around $3k / month. Then $5k / month. Then $10k / month"</em> —
    and in February 2013, after a month that had reached $20,000, an application made <em>"on a
    whim"</em> put them in an accelerator, where <em>"we raised our seed
    round of almost $1M."</em> The peak, by his account, was <em>"getting to $30k in monthly
    revenue."</em></p>

    <p>The money was for marketing and sales, which the two founders — <em>"product guys
    first"</em>, by his account — knew they lacked. Five of the hires he lists — an enterprise
    marketer, a cold-calling salesperson, a developer evangelist, a former editor, an SEO and
    content-marketing company — <em>"None of those panned out, for
    various reasons."</em> The good hires they did make <em>"were not at the positions we needed to
    really make a difference."</em> They ran the experiments a business runs when the growth will
    not come: affiliates, discounts, purchase orders, custom work, retargeting, landing pages,
    lifecycle emails, live chat. <em>"Most of the stuff we did had a positive effect, but none had
    the breakout potential we had hoped. We were stuck in a rot."</em></p>

    <p>Then the position, in his words. <em>"The timeline now is
    Sept. 2014, a little over a year since we raised our seed round."</em> <em>"We had spent a
    little over half the money we raised, which was a healthy rate, but only when you are
    growing."</em> They chose one more big move: a full pivot, to become <em>"the "Shopify for
    digital products""</em>, built in around three months, Galperin <em>"programming for 10-12 hours a day"</em>, and
    launched in January 2015 with press and a campaign. <em>"But as time went by, nothing changed."</em></p>

    <p>Then the months, in one paragraph: <em>"As the months went by, it was becoming clear we
    had exhausted every option. We had no more ideas on how to grow the company. We had lost. It
    took us a while to come to terms with this reality. We didn't bring it up when we talked –
    instead, it was always ‘what more can we do?’. But eventually, we had that talk and faced the
    music. We had given 5 years of our lives to the company, we had some successes, but we had
    taken it as far as we could. It was time to move on."</em></p>

    <p>And then the stop itself. <em>"Our immediate
    goals were to return as much as we could to our investors. We tried shopping the company for a
    while, and while there was significant interest at one point, no one was able to
    commit."</em> <em>"Eventually we settled for selling the assets only (site and code-base),
    while dissolving the company. Definitely not the outcome we were looking for."</em> What the account gives is the order: the decision, then the attempt to sell
    the whole, then the sale of what could be sold, then the formal dissolution — and, at the end,
    the investors thanked, several by name. Five years, ended by the people who owned it, and late
    by their own reckoning: the pivot, he writes afterwards, <em>"was brought on too late to have a
    chance to really succeed."</em></p>
  </section>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 3</span> The reasoning</h2>

    <h3>Exhausted options are a date, not a feeling</h3>

    <p><strong>A business has exhausted its options when the last big move has had its time and
    not moved the figures — and the date on which that is judged has to be written before the
    move is made, because afterwards every month is "what more can we do?"</strong> Binpress had
    the date in the record and did not use it: September 2014, half the money gone at a rate that
    was healthy only for a growing company, and the pivot chosen instead of the talk. The pivot
    was not wrong. Choosing it without a date was — because the months after January 2015 were
    spent not bringing it up, and the account says so.</p>

    <p>This is <a href="20-managing-people.html">chapter twenty</a>'s forcing function pointed at
    the business instead of at a person, and it is not a new rule. The sentence you wrote in
    <a href="16-enough.html">chapter sixteen</a> already ends in four consequences — raise prices,
    cut the offer, take contract work, stop — and the first three are the big moves. What this
    chapter adds is that each of them gets a date of its own: <em>if [the move] has not moved [the
    figure] by [the date], the next consequence on the list is the one you take</em> — and when the
    list is used up, the one left is stop, and the procedure in the next section is how it is
    carried out. The figure is sixteen's throw-off or one of
    <a href="22-measuring-the-right-three-things.html">chapter twenty-two</a>'s three, chosen
    before the move. The date is when the move has had a fair run — a quarter, two — and not
    longer, because the thing being spent while you wait is the reserve, and it is what pays for
    stopping well.</p>

    <p>The boundary is the one every chapter in this part has drawn: a business can stop growing
    and be fine. <a href="16-enough.html">Chapter sixteen</a>'s Pinboard did not grow for years and paid its
    owner; a business whose sentence is still being met is not in this chapter, and a single miss
    is not an instruction to open it. This chapter is for the business whose figures are falling or whose owner is
    funding it from a reserve that is running out — the case where going on has a price and the
    price is being paid without a decision. <a href="26-when-the-ground-moves.html">Chapter twenty-six</a>'s shock that does not lift
    arrives here the same way, through sixteen's sentence, and only when its last consequence is
    the one left.</p>

    <h3>Stop in order</h3>

    <p>Binpress's order is the one this book takes as general. First the decision, made out loud,
    between the people who own it. Then the attempt to sell the whole — a going concern is usually
    worth more than its parts, and finding out whether anyone will pay for the whole cost Binpress
    <em>"a while"</em> and nothing the account counts. Then, if that
    fails, the parts: what can be sold is sold, before it stops being worth anything. Then the
    people who are owed — investors in Binpress's case; for most readers a bank, a family, a
    landlord, suppliers, and the staff <a href="20-managing-people.html">chapter twenty</a>'s box
    covers — accounted to, in an order that is not yours to choose and, on Dutch ground, is in the box
    below. Then the formal end:
    dissolved, deregistered, filed, so that the business is over rather than merely stopped.
    <strong>The cost of stopping badly is not the loss. It is the loss plus everything that was
    still worth something on the day the decision was avoided, plus what an owner who skipped the formal end can be asked to answer for afterwards, which the
    box quotes.</strong></p>

    <div class="ground">
      <h3>On Dutch ground · winding up cleanly</h3>

      <p>If you are not in the Netherlands, skip to Monday. If you are, the formal end has two
      routes, and which one is yours turns on legal form — the fork <a href="12-reading-your-own-business.html">chapter
      twelve</a> and <a href="15-paying-yourself.html">chapter fifteen</a> met. Read the fork first; the steps below are meaningless attached to the wrong side of it. A
      partnership is on neither side — <em lang="nl">"De turboliquidatie kun je niet gebruiken voor
      het beëindigen van een eenmanszaak of vennootschap onder firma (vof)"</em> — and a vof's end is
      the jurist's question from the start.</p>

      <p><strong>Trading in your own name</strong> — an eenmanszaak — the end is a deregistration
      from the Handelsregister, done online: <em lang="nl">"Stop je met je eenmanszaak? Dan moet je
      deze uitschrijven bij KVK. Dit regel je online met je DigiD. Het uitschrijven is
      gratis."</em> It is final — <em lang="nl">"Een uitschrijving is definitief en heeft
      gevolgen"</em> — and there is no pause: <em lang="nl">"Tijdelijk uitschrijven kan niet."</em>
      You choose the date, and by it you must have stopped trading. The Chamber tells the tax
      authority for you — <em lang="nl">"Wij geven je uitschrijving door aan de
      Belastingdienst."</em> — but the returns do not stop with the business: <em lang="nl">"Blijf
      je aangiftes doen zolang deze in Mijn Belastingdienst Zakelijk staan."</em> The Belastingdienst
      writes to say when the last VAT return is due.</p>

      <p><strong>A bv or other legal person</strong> ends by dissolution, and the fast route is the
      turboliquidatie — available on one condition: <em lang="nl">"Er mogen geen baten
      (bezittingen) meer zijn op het moment van ontbinding."</em> <em lang="nl">"Als er geen
      bezittingen zijn op het moment van ontbinding, dan stop je direct met je bedrijf."</em> The
      condition is narrower than it sounds, and the Chamber says so: <em lang="nl">"Het begrip
      baten omvat meer dan je misschien zou denken."</em> Money the business is still owed counts
      — <em lang="nl">"Daarnaast zijn vorderingen ook baten"</em> — including amounts you could
      collect if you pursued them. Where there are assets at the moment of dissolution the fast route is closed — <em
      lang="nl">"Dan is een turboliquidatie niet mogelijk"</em>; <em lang="nl">"Je moet dan de
      wettelijke vereffeningsprocedure volgen"</em> — and the Chamber describes one way to reach it:
      sell everything and pay the creditors first, <em lang="nl">"zoveel mogelijk"</em>, and <em
      lang="nl">"Hierbij houd je rekening met de belangen en rangorde van schuldeisers"</em>, so
      that no baten remain when the resolution is passed. Where the debts are many, its guidance is
      to consider bankruptcy instead, with a curator:
      <em lang="nl">"Zijn er veel schulden? Dan kun je overwegen om geen turboliquidatie te doen,
      en in plaats daarvan een faillissement aan te vragen."</em> Running contracts — a lease, an
      employment — are a reason not to use the route at all: <em lang="nl">"Doe je dit wel, dan
      loop je het risico dat je als bestuurder persoonlijk aansprakelijk wordt."</em> And the route carries a filing duty since the temporary transparency law in force from 15
      November 2023 — <em lang="nl">"Binnen veertien dagen na het ontbindingsbesluit moet het bestuur
      verschillende (financiële) documenten indienen bij ons."</em> — after which the creditors are
      told: <em lang="nl">"Nadat alles is ingediend, stelt het bestuur schuldeisers schriftelijk op
      de hoogte van de turboliquidatie."</em> Misuse is the Chamber's own warning: a creditor can ask
      the court to re-liquidate, a director who paid some creditors ahead of others or hid baten can
      be held personally liable, and a bestuursverbod is possible.</p>

      <p>None of this is advice, and the Chamber's own instruction is the one this box repeats:
      <em lang="nl">"Vraag advies van een (bedrijfs)jurist en accountant voordat er een
      ontbindingsbesluit volgt."</em> For a sole trader, the accountant, before the date is chosen.
      For a bv with anything still owed to it or by it, the accountant and a jurist before the
      resolution to dissolve, and a curator if the debts say so. This box quotes the procedure; it
      does not say what your baten are, what your creditors' order is, or what you personally
      answer for if the route is misused.</p>

      <span class="checked">Checked 28 August 2026 against KVK's pages "Eenmanszaak uitschrijven"
      and "Een rechtspersoon beëindigen via de turboliquidatie" (the latter updated 4 August 2026),
      both consulted 6 August 2026. The underlying statute — Book 2 of the Civil Code on
      dissolution and liquidation, and the tijdelijke Wet transparantie turboliquidatie — is not quoted here;
      the Chamber's guidance is, and the jurist is for the rest.</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="monday">
    <h2>Monday</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Take the sentence from chapter sixteen and give its next consequence a date.</strong> One line: <em>if [the move] has not moved [sixteen's throw-off or one of chapter twenty-two's figures] to [a number] by [a date], we take the next consequence on the list that month.</em> Date it, sign it with whoever owns the business with you, and put it where the weekly numbers are read. When the list has one item left, this chapter is the month.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the reserve against the decision, not the month.</strong> Take chapter thirteen's cash — the bank balance less what is held for the tax authority — and divide it by the gap between a month's outgoings, counted as chapter sixteen counted them, and this month's takings. The answer is months. If the date in item 1 is later than that, the date is wrong, not the reserve: bring it forward, because stopping well costs money and it has to be there on the day.</li>
      <li><strong>Price the parts now, while they are worth something.</strong> One page: the customer list, the equipment, the lease, the name, the code, the stock — what each would fetch this quarter, and from whom. You are not selling. You are finding out what the decision would cost to delay.</li>
      <li><strong>List who is owed, and in what order.</strong> The bank, the family, the landlord, the suppliers, the staff, the tax authority — with the amounts and, on Dutch ground, the order the jurist gives you. This is the list Binpress's founders called their immediate goal, and it is written before the talk, not after.</li>
      <li><strong>On the date, have the talk, and say one of two things.</strong> <em>The rule held, and here is the next move and its date</em>, or <em>the rule did not hold, and here is the order: whole, then parts, then the list in item 4, then the filing.</em> A third thing — <em>let's give it another quarter</em> — is only allowed with a new number and a new date written down, and once.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <div class="owed">
    <p class="case-head"><span class="who">What would prove this chapter wrong</span></p>

    <p>What is owed is the business that wrote the end-rule, hit the date, had the talk, stopped
    in order — and was wrong: the one that would have turned in the quarter after it closed, on
    the move it had already made, for a reason that was visible in hindsight and not on the day.
    Binpress is not that case; its founders stopped late by their own account, and nothing they
    report suggests another quarter would have found the growth that four years had not. The
    inverse is the common one and it is not a counter-case: businesses that went on past every
    option until the stop was made for them, by a bank or a court, and lost the parts as well as
    the whole. The true counter-case is nearly unwritable, because an owner who stopped on a rule
    and later saw what they might have had does not publish the rule. Until it appears, the
    asymmetry stands where the whole book has left it: a rule with a date costs one conversation
    and can be wrong once; the absence of one costs the reserve, the parts, and the people, and is
    wrong every month it is not made.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Reading</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="src">Source ·</span> <a href="https://erangalperin.com/2016/02/11/startup-postmortem-binpress/">Eran Galperin, "Startup Postmortem: Binpress"</a>, 11 February 2016 — the launch and the ten users, the contest, the revenue ladder, the accelerator and the seed round, the five hires, the marketing experiments, the September 2014 position, the pivot and the January 2015 launch, the "what more can we do?" months, the decision, the attempt to sell the company, the asset sale and the dissolution.</li>
      <li><span class="src">Source ·</span> <a href="https://www.kvk.nl/stoppen-en-uitschrijven/eenmanszaak-uitschrijven/">KVK, "Eenmanszaak uitschrijven"</a> and <a href="https://www.kvk.nl/stoppen-en-uitschrijven/een-rechtspersoon-beeindigen-via-de-turboliquidatie/">"Een rechtspersoon beëindigen via de turboliquidatie"</a>, consulted 6 August 2026 — the deregistration procedure for a sole trader and the conditions, duties and warnings attached to the fast-track dissolution of a legal person.</li>
      <li><span class="src">Note ·</span> A founder's own retrospective, written months after the outcome and with its shape — the weaker evidence class this book has attached to every Galperin source, and his sixth appearance in it, on a different company from the other five. Binpress was accelerator- and seed-funded, with a hired team; that is a better-resourced position than most readers of this book start from, and the chapter takes from it the order of the stop, not the scale. No figure is given for what investors recovered, the amount raised is "almost $1M" and not a filing, and the page's metadata records a later modification date this book did not compare against the original. The KVK pages quote procedure, not the statute beneath it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="part-close">
    <h2>What the book asked</h2>
    <p>Five questions, in an order that could not be changed: is there anything here; will someone
    pay; does the money work; can it run without you; will it last. Each one was answered from businesses that published enough to be checked — three times from
    a filing or a statute, where no business had — and each answer came with the case that would
    have proved it wrong: found, or owed and said so. Most of those businesses sold software,
    because those are the owners who write their numbers down; the <a href="../about.html">method
    page</a> says what that leaves out, and it is not small. The last question has the same shape as
    the first. Chapter four asked you to write, before you had anything, what would make you stop.
    This one asks the same of a business that has become most of your life, and the answer is the
    same instrument: a number, a date, and a consequence you chose while you could still choose.
    Everything between the two was about earning the right to need it late.</p>
  </section>

