"localeString":"nl","contentType":"kvkwebsite:pageDocument","systemCreationDate":1662718391552,"systemModificationDate":1777386311919,"systemPublicationDate":1777386877796,"id":"7f5d92f4-394e-46e9-8461-b2bb9c00a97d"}},"uf6c9008b4e994459a13fb3d511645893":{"type":"document","links":{"site":{"href":"/over-kvk/blijf-op-de-hoogte/","type":"internal"}},"meta":{},"data":{"name":"blijf-op-de-hoogte","displayName":"Blijf op de hoogte","seo":{"name":"kvkwebsite:seo","displayName":"kvkwebsite:seo","seotitle":"Meld je aan voor het laatste ondernemersnieuws","seodescription":"Blijf op de hoogte van belangrijk nieuws en nuttige tips voor ondernemers. Kies zelf de onderwerpen waarover je meer informatie wilt ontvangen.","seonoindex":true,"contentType":"seosupport:seo"},"teamEnglish":{"name":"kvkwebsite:teamEnglish","displayName":"kvkwebsite:teamEnglish","actionTeamEnglish":"5","changesTeamEnglish":false,"contentType":"kvkwebsite:teamEnglish"},"chatbotSettings":null,"showPlaceholderInXM":false,"intro":"","title":"","localeString":"nl","contentType":"kvkwebsite:pageDocument","systemCreationDate":1761635672731,"systemModificationDate":1761635710595,"systemPublicationDate":1761635716803,"id":"f6c9008b-4e99-4459-a13f-b3d511645893"}},"u2e4614a0eb8241908b949ef5801d3415":{"type":"imageset","links":{},"meta":{},"data":{"name":"prent-footer.svg","displayName":"prent-footer.svg","fileName":"prent-footer.svg","description":null,"thumbnail":{"name":"hippogallery:thumbnail","displayName":"hippogallery:thumbnail","width":60,"height":60,"lastModified":1684227169581,"mimeType":"image/svg+xml","filename":"prent-footer.svg","contentType":"hippogallery:image","size":36917,"links":{"site":{"href":"https://production-site-nl.kvk.bloomreach.cloud/binaries/thumbnail/content/gallery/kvkwebsite-nl/componenten/tekstdocumenten/svg-afbeeldingen/prent-footer.svg","type":"resource"}}},"original":{"name":"hippogallery:original","displayName":"hippogallery:original","width":0,"height":0,"lastModified":1684227169581,"mimeType":"image/svg+xml","filename":null,"contentType":"hippogallery:image","size":36917,"links":{"site":{"href":"https://production-site-nl.kvk.bloomreach.cloud/binaries/content/gallery/kvkwebsite-nl/componenten/tekstdocumenten/svg-afbeeldingen/prent-footer.svg","type":"resource"}}},"contentType":"kvkwebsite:svgImageSet","localeString":null,"id":"2e4614a0-eb82-4190-8b94-9ef5801d3415"}}}},"_sentryTraceData":"b6d4e0f13bf33c90821bbc1edf839ccc-ec7f48c9b7828318-0","_sentryBaggage":"sentry-environment=production,sentry-release=1dd06929543cf0f7e4b7d2b90491ba711b646e3b,sentry-public_key=dfa2381926f54ce79df1ee6882b0144f,sentry-trace_id=b6d4e0f13bf33c90821bbc1edf839ccc,sentry-org_id=302809,sentry-sampled=false,sentry-sample_rand=0.5880480758248704,sentry-sample_rate=0.002"},"__N_SSP":true},"page":"/[[...routes]]","query":{"routes":["stoppen-en-uitschrijven","een-rechtspersoon-beeindigen-via-de-turboliquidatie"]},"buildId":"2.147.0-1dd06929","isFallback":false,"isExperimentalCompile":false,"dynamicIds":[36259,79856,74200,73526],"gssp":true,"locale":"nl","locales":["nl","en"],"defaultLocale":"nl","scriptLoader":[]}</script></body></html>    65	    It is whether going on is a decision or the absence of one</strong> — and if you cannot say
    66	    what would have to happen, by when, for the answer to be <em>stop</em>, it is the absence.</p>
    67	  </section>
    68	
    69	  <section class="move">
    70	    <h2><span class="num">Move 2</span> What actually happened</h2>
    71	
    72	    <p>Before Gymdesk, Eran Galperin had another company, and this is its ending. Binpress, a marketplace for developers' code libraries, was
    73	    conceived in late 2010 by Galperin and his business partner, Adam, out of a web-development
    74	    shop they had run for four years; it soft-launched at the end of that year, and <em>"Over the
    75	    course of 2 months, we had exactly 10 new users."</em> A contest with $40,000 in sponsored
    76	    prizes seeded the marketplace with around two hundred products; sales then ran at <em>"around $500 in sales"</em> a month for <em>"the first several
    77	    months"</em>. Mobile components changed that —
    78	    <em>"Our revenue stabilized around $3k / month. Then $5k / month. Then $10k / month"</em> —
    79	    and in February 2013, after a month that had reached $20,000, an application made <em>"on a
    80	    whim"</em> put them in an accelerator, where <em>"we raised our seed
    81	    round of almost $1M."</em> The peak, by his account, was <em>"getting to $30k in monthly
    82	    revenue."</em></p>
    83	
    84	    <p>The money was for marketing and sales, which the two founders — <em>"product guys
    85	    first"</em>, by his account — knew they lacked. Five of the hires he lists — an enterprise
    86	    marketer, a cold-calling salesperson, a developer evangelist, a former editor, an SEO and
    87	    content-marketing company — <em>"None of those panned out, for
    88	    various reasons."</em> The good hires they did make <em>"were not at the positions we needed to
    89	    really make a difference."</em> They ran the experiments a business runs when the growth will
    90	    not come: affiliates, discounts, purchase orders, custom work, retargeting, landing pages,
    91	    lifecycle emails, live chat. <em>"Most of the stuff we did had a positive effect, but none had
    92	    the breakout potential we had hoped. We were stuck in a rot."</em></p>
    93	
    94	    <p>Then the position, in his words. <em>"The timeline now is
    95	    Sept. 2014, a little over a year since we raised our seed round."</em> <em>"We had spent a
    96	    little over half the money we raised, which was a healthy rate, but only when you are
    97	    growing."</em> They chose one more big move: a full pivot, to become <em>"the "Shopify for
    98	    digital products""</em>, built in around three months, Galperin <em>"programming for 10-12 hours a day"</em>, and
    99	    launched in January 2015 with press and a campaign. <em>"But as time went by, nothing changed."</em></p>
   100	
   101	    <p>Then the months, in one paragraph: <em>"As the months went by, it was becoming clear we
   102	    had exhausted every option. We had no more ideas on how to grow the company. We had lost. It
   103	    took us a while to come to terms with this reality. We didn't bring it up when we talked –
   104	    instead, it was always ‘what more can we do?’. But eventually, we had that talk and faced the
   105	    music. We had given 5 years of our lives to the company, we had some successes, but we had
   106	    taken it as far as we could. It was time to move on."</em></p>
   107	
   108	    <p>And then the stop itself. <em>"Our immediate
   109	    goals were to return as much as we could to our investors. We tried shopping the company for a
   110	    while, and while there was significant interest at one point, no one was able to
   111	    commit."</em> <em>"Eventually we settled for selling the assets only (site and code-base),
   112	    while dissolving the company. Definitely not the outcome we were looking for."</em> What the account gives is the order: the decision, then the attempt to sell
   113	    the whole, then the sale of what could be sold, then the formal dissolution — and, at the end,
   114	    the investors thanked, several by name. Five years, ended by the people who owned it, and late
   115	    by their own reckoning: the pivot, he writes afterwards, <em>"was brought on too late to have a
   116	    chance to really succeed."</em></p>
   117	  </section>
   118	
   119	  <section class="move">
   120	    <h2><span class="num">Move 3</span> The reasoning</h2>
   121	
   122	    <h3>Exhausted options are a date, not a feeling</h3>
   123	
   124	    <p><strong>A business has exhausted its options when the last big move has had its time and
   125	    not moved the figures — and the date on which that is judged has to be written before the
   126	    move is made, because afterwards every month is "what more can we do?"</strong> Binpress had
   127	    the date in the record and did not use it: September 2014, half the money gone at a rate that
   128	    was healthy only for a growing company, and the pivot chosen instead of the talk. The pivot
   129	    was not wrong. Choosing it without a date was — because the months after January 2015 were
   130	    spent not bringing it up, and the account says so.</p>
   131	
   132	    <p>This is <a href="20-managing-people.html">chapter twenty</a>'s forcing function pointed at
   133	    the business instead of at a person, and it is not a new rule. The sentence you wrote in
   134	    <a href="16-enough.html">chapter sixteen</a> already ends in four consequences — raise prices,
   135	    cut the offer, take contract work, stop — and the first three are the big moves. What this
   136	    chapter adds is that each of them gets a date of its own: <em>if [the move] has not moved [the
   137	    figure] by [the date], the next consequence on the list is the one you take</em> — and when the
   138	    list is used up, the one left is stop, and the procedure in the next section is how it is
   139	    carried out. The figure is sixteen's throw-off or one of
   140	    <a href="22-measuring-the-right-three-things.html">chapter twenty-two</a>'s three, chosen
   141	    before the move. The date is when the move has had a fair run — a quarter, two — and not
   142	    longer, because the thing being spent while you wait is the reserve, and it is what pays for
   143	    stopping well.</p>
   144	
   145	    <p>The boundary is the one every chapter in this part has drawn: a business can stop growing
   146	    and be fine. <a href="16-enough.html">Chapter sixteen</a>'s Pinboard did not grow for years and paid its
   147	    owner; a business whose sentence is still being met is not in this chapter, and a single miss
   148	    is not an instruction to open it. This chapter is for the business whose figures are falling or whose owner is
   149	    funding it from a reserve that is running out — the case where going on has a price and the
   150	    price is being paid without a decision. <a href="26-when-the-ground-moves.html">Chapter twenty-six</a>'s shock that does not lift
   151	    arrives here the same way, through sixteen's sentence, and only when its last consequence is
   152	    the one left.</p>
   153	
   154	    <h3>Stop in order</h3>
   155	
   156	    <p>Binpress's order is the one this book takes as general. First the decision, made out loud,
   157	    between the people who own it. Then the attempt to sell the whole — a going concern is usually
   158	    worth more than its parts, and finding out whether anyone will pay for the whole cost Binpress
   159	    <em>"a while"</em> and nothing the account counts. Then, if that
   160	    fails, the parts: what can be sold is sold, before it stops being worth anything. Then the
   161	    people who are owed — investors in Binpress's case; for most readers a bank, a family, a
   162	    landlord, suppliers, and the staff <a href="20-managing-people.html">chapter twenty</a>'s box
   163	    covers — accounted to, in an order that is not yours to choose and, on Dutch ground, is in the box
   164	    below. Then the formal end:
   165	    dissolved, deregistered, filed, so that the business is over rather than merely stopped.
   166	    <strong>The cost of stopping badly is not the loss. It is the loss plus everything that was
   167	    still worth something on the day the decision was avoided, plus what an owner who skipped the formal end can be asked to answer for afterwards, which the
   168	    box quotes.</strong></p>
   169	
   170	    <div class="ground">
   171	      <h3>On Dutch ground · winding up cleanly</h3>
   172	
   173	      <p>If you are not in the Netherlands, skip to Monday. If you are, the formal end has two
   174	      routes, and which one is yours turns on legal form — the fork <a href="12-reading-your-own-business.html">chapter
   175	      twelve</a> and <a href="15-paying-yourself.html">chapter fifteen</a> met. Read the fork first; the steps below are meaningless attached to the wrong side of it. A
   176	      partnership is on neither side — <em lang="nl">"De turboliquidatie kun je niet gebruiken voor
   177	      het beëindigen van een eenmanszaak of vennootschap onder firma (vof)"</em> — and a vof's end is
   178	      the jurist's question from the start.</p>
   179	
   180	      <p><strong>Trading in your own name</strong> — an eenmanszaak — the end is a deregistration
   181	      from the Handelsregister, done online: <em lang="nl">"Stop je met je eenmanszaak? Dan moet je
   182	      deze uitschrijven bij KVK. Dit regel je online met je DigiD. Het uitschrijven is
   183	      gratis."</em> It is final — <em lang="nl">"Een uitschrijving is definitief en heeft
   184	      gevolgen"</em> — and there is no pause: <em lang="nl">"Tijdelijk uitschrijven kan niet."</em>
   185	      You choose the date, and by it you must have stopped trading. The Chamber tells the tax
   186	      authority for you — <em lang="nl">"Wij geven je uitschrijving door aan de
   187	      Belastingdienst."</em> — but the returns do not stop with the business: <em lang="nl">"Blijf
   188	      je aangiftes doen zolang deze in Mijn Belastingdienst Zakelijk staan."</em> The Belastingdienst
   189	      writes to say when the last VAT return is due.</p>
   190	
   191	      <p><strong>A bv or other legal person</strong> ends by dissolution, and the fast route is the
   192	      turboliquidatie — available on one condition: <em lang="nl">"Er mogen geen baten
   193	      (bezittingen) meer zijn op het moment van ontbinding."</em> <em lang="nl">"Als er geen
   194	      bezittingen zijn op het moment van ontbinding, dan stop je direct met je bedrijf."</em> The
   195	      condition is narrower than it sounds, and the Chamber says so: <em lang="nl">"Het begrip
   196	      baten omvat meer dan je misschien zou denken."</em> Money the business is still owed counts
   197	      — <em lang="nl">"Daarnaast zijn vorderingen ook baten"</em> — including amounts you could
   198	      collect if you pursued them. Where there are assets at the moment of dissolution the fast route is closed — <em
   199	      lang="nl">"Dan is een turboliquidatie niet mogelijk"</em>; <em lang="nl">"Je moet dan de
   200	      wettelijke vereffeningsprocedure volgen"</em> — and the Chamber describes one way to reach it:
   201	      sell everything and pay the creditors first, <em lang="nl">"zoveel mogelijk"</em>, and <em
   202	      lang="nl">"Hierbij houd je rekening met de belangen en rangorde van schuldeisers"</em>, so
   203	      that no baten remain when the resolution is passed. Where the debts are many, its guidance is
   204	      to consider bankruptcy instead, with a curator:
   205	      <em lang="nl">"Zijn er veel schulden? Dan kun je overwegen om geen turboliquidatie te doen,
   206	      en in plaats daarvan een faillissement aan te vragen."</em> Running contracts — a lease, an
   207	      employment — are a reason not to use the route at all: <em lang="nl">"Doe je dit wel, dan
   208	      loop je het risico dat je als bestuurder persoonlijk aansprakelijk wordt."</em> And the route carries a filing duty since the temporary transparency law in force from 15
   209	      November 2023 — <em lang="nl">"Binnen veertien dagen na het ontbindingsbesluit moet het bestuur
   210	      verschillende (financiële) documenten indienen bij ons."</em> — after which the creditors are

